class="enc-name">Mutahharten</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutahharten was the ruler of Erzincan in eastern Anatolia in the late 14th and early 15th centuries, after serving as a governor under the Eretnids.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutambuka"><span class="enc-name">Mutambuka</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutambuka was Omugabe, or king, of Nkore in the mid-nineteenth century, one of the rulers under whom the kingdom became an expanding power.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Mutamid ibn Abbad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mutamid ibn Abbad (1040–1095) was the last Abbadid king of the Taifa of Seville, ruling from 1069 to 1091, and one of the finest poets of al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutapa-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutapa Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mutapa Empire, also known as the Mwene Mutapa state, was a Shona kingdom that emerged in the mid-fifteenth century between the Zambezi and Limpopo rivers, in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutara-ii-rwogera"><span class="enc-name">Mutara II Rwogera</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutara II Rwogera was mwami, or king, of Rwanda in the first half of the 19th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutara-iii-rudahigwa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutara-iii-rudahigwa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutara III Rudahigwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutara III Rudahigwa was mwami (king) of Rwanda from 1931 to 1959, installed by the Belgian administration of Ruanda-Urundi in place of his father.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutare"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutare.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutare</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutare, called Umtali until 1982, is a city in eastern Zimbabwe near the border with Mozambique.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutasa"><span class="enc-name">Mutasa</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutasa is the hereditary title of the rulers of Manyika, a Shona kingdom in the highlands on the border of present-day Zimbabwe and Mozambique.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mutawakkil-ismail"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mutawakkil-ismail.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Mutawakkil Isma'il</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mutawakkil Isma'il was the Zaydi imam of Yemen from 1644 to 1676 and the most powerful ruler of the Qasimid state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mutawakkil-yahya-sharaf-al-din"><span class="enc-name">Al-Mutawakkil Yahya Sharaf al-Din</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mutawakkil Yahya Sharaf al-Din was Zaydi imam of Yemen from 1506 to 1555.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutesa-i-of-buganda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutesa-i-of-buganda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutesa I of Buganda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutesa I was kabaka of Buganda from 1856 to 1884.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutesa-ii-of-buganda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutesa-ii-of-buganda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutesa II of Buganda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutesa II was kabaka of Buganda from 1939.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-muti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Muti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Muti was the Abbasid caliph from 946 to 974, placed on the throne by the Buyid ruler Mu'izz al-Dawla after the blinding and deposition of al-Mustakfi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutimir-of-serbia"><span class="enc-name">Mutimir of Serbia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutimir was Prince of Serbia in the second half of the 9th century, a member of the Vlastimirović dynasty and son of Vlastimir.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutomboko"><span class="enc-name">Mutomboko</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mutomboko is an annual ceremony of the Lunda people of the Luapula valley in Zambia, held each July at Mwansabombwe, the seat of the Mwata Kazembe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutsamudu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutsamudu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutsamudu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutsamudu is the capital of the island of Anjouan in the Comoros and the country's second-largest town.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-munemitsu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutsu-munemitsu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutsu Munemitsu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutsu Munemitsu was a Japanese statesman from the Kii domain who served as foreign minister from 1892 to 1896.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mutsu-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mutsu Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mutsu Province was an old province of Japan covering most of northern Honshu, the largest in the country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muwaffaq"><span class="enc-name">Al-Muwaffaq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Muwaffaq was an Abbasid prince, son of the caliph al-Mutawakkil, who held real power during the reign of his brother al-Mu'tamid from the 870s until his death in 891.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii"><span class="enc-name">Muwatalli II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muwatalli II was a king of the Hittite Empire in the early thirteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muyeol-of-silla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muyeol-of-silla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muyeol of Silla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muyeol, born Kim Chunchu, was king of Silla from 654 to 661, the first king from the 'true bone' rank.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muza"><span class="enc-name">Muza</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Muza was an ancient port on the Red Sea coast of south-western Arabia, near modern Mocha in Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri was the ruler of Erbil in northern Iraq from 1190 to 1233, son of Zayn al-Din Ali Kuchuk.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-jung"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muzaffar-jung.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muzaffar Jung</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muzaffar Jung was a grandson of Asaf Jah I who claimed the throne of Hyderabad after 1748 with French support.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muzaffar-shah-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muzaffar Shah I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muzaffar Shah I, born Zafar Khan, was the founder of the Gujarat Sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i-of-perak"><span class="enc-name">Muzaffar Shah I of Perak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muzaffar Shah I was the first sultan of Perak, reigning from about 1528 to 1549.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-of-malacca"><span class="enc-name">Muzaffar Shah of Malacca</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muzaffar Shah was sultan of Malacca from about 1445 to 1459, the first of its rulers to use the title sultan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muzaffar-yusuf-i"><span class="enc-name">Al-Muzaffar Yusuf I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Muzaffar Yusuf I was the second sultan of the Rasulid dynasty of Yemen, reigning from 1249 to 1295.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muzaka-family.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muzaka family</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Muzaka were an Albanian noble family of the Myzeqe plain and Berat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muziris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muziris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muziris</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Muziris was an ancient port on the Malabar Coast of south-western India, probably near modern Pattanam in Kerala.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muzo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muzo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Muzo is a town in the department of Boyacá, Colombia, in the western foothills of the Eastern Cordillera.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mua-tonga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muʻa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Muʻa is a town on the shore of the lagoon of Tongatapu, the main island of Tonga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwaant-yaav"><span class="enc-name">Mwaant Yaav</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwaant Yaav (also written Mwata Yamvo) is the title of the paramount ruler of the Lunda Empire in central Africa, taken by each king from the late seventeenth century…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwaant-yaav-i"><span class="enc-name">Mwaant Yaav I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwaant Yaav I was a ruler of the Lunda Empire in central Africa, associated with the period c. 1665–1700.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwai-kibaki"><span class="enc-name">Mwai Kibaki</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwai Kibaki was president of Kenya from 2002 to 2013.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwamba-bemba-title"><span class="enc-name">Mwamba (Bemba title)</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwamba is the title of one of the senior chiefs of the Bemba of north-eastern Zambia, who rules a large territory next to that of the Chitimukulu, the paramount.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mwambutsa-iv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mwambutsa IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwambutsa IV was mwami (king) of Burundi from 1915 to 1966, reigning under Belgian rule and into independence in 1962.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwana-pwo"><span class="enc-name">Mwana Pwo</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwana Pwo, &quot;young woman&quot;, is a type of wooden dance mask of the Chokwe people of Angola, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Zambia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwanawina-iii"><span class="enc-name">Mwanawina III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwanawina III was Litunga, king of the Lozi of Barotseland, from 1948 until his death in 1968, and a son of Lewanika.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mwanga-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mwanga II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwanga II was kabaka (king) of Buganda from 1884, succeeding Mutesa I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwari-cult"><span class="enc-name">Mwari cult</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mwari cult is a Shona religious tradition centred on the high god Mwari, with oracular shrines in the Matopos Hills of present-day Zimbabwe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwata-yamvo"><span class="enc-name">Mwata Yamvo</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwata Yamvo is the title of the paramount ruler of the Lunda Empire, centred in the south of the present Democratic Republic of the Congo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwembe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mwembe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mwembe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwembe was the town of the Yao chiefs called Mataka on the highlands east of Lake Malawi, in what is now Niassa province of northern Mozambique.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwene-mutapa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mwene-mutapa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mwene Mutapa</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwene Mutapa was the title of the king of the Mutapa Empire, a term from which the state itself took its name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mwezi-iv-gisabo"><span class="enc-name">Mwezi IV Gisabo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mwezi IV Gisabo was mwami (king) of Burundi from about 1852 to 1908, the last to rule before effective colonial control.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/my-chau"><span class="enc-name">Mỵ Châu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mỵ Châu is a princess of Vietnamese legend, daughter of King An Dương Vương of Âu Lạc.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/my-son"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/my-son.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mỹ Sơn</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mỹ Sơn is a group of ruined Hindu temples in a valley of Quảng Nam province, central Vietnam, built by the kings of Champa from the 4th to the 13th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/my-tho"><span class="enc-name">My Tho</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">My Tho is a city in the Mekong delta of southern Vietnam, capital of Tien Giang province, on the northern branch of the Mekong.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/myanmar-civil-war-2021-present"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/myanmar-civil-war-2021-present.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Myanmar civil war (2021–present)</span><span class="enc-type">War · 2021–present</span><span class="enc-snip">The Myanmar civil war is the armed conflict that followed the military coup of February 2021, in which the army overthrew Aung San Suu Kyi's elected government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/myitnge-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/myitnge-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Myitnge River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Myitnge, or Dokhtawady, is a river of central Myanmar that rises in the Shan Hills and flows west to join the Irrawaddy just south of Mandalay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mykhailo-hrushevsky"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mykhailo-hrushevsky.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mykhailo Hrushevsky</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mykhailo Hrushevsky was a Ukrainian historian, author of a multi-volume History of Ukraine-Rus', and a leader of the national movement.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mykola-ryabovol"><span class="enc-name">Mykola Ryabovol</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mykola Ryabovol was a Kuban Cossack politician and chairman of the Kuban Rada in the Kuban People's Republic during the Russian Civil War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mykola-skrypnyk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mykola Skrypnyk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mykola Skrypnyk was a Ukrainian Bolshevik who served in the People's Secretariat of the Soviet Ukrainian government formed in Kharkiv in December 1917.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mykonos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mykonos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mykonos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mykonos is a small island of the Cyclades in the Aegean Sea, Greece.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mylae"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mylae.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mylae</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mylae was an ancient Greek city on the north coast of Sicily, founded by settlers from Zancle in the 8th century BCE on the site of modern Milazzo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mynyddog-mwynfawr"><span class="enc-name">Mynyddog Mwynfawr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mynyddog Mwynfawr was a ruler of the Gododdin, the Brittonic people of south-east Scotland, known from the early Welsh poem Y Gododdin attributed to Aneirin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/myocheong"><span class="enc-name">Myocheong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Myocheong was a Korean Buddhist monk and geomancer of the Goryeo period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/myos-hormos"><span class="enc-name">Myos Hormos</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Myos Hormos was a port on the Red Sea coast of Egypt, at Quseir al-Qadim north of modern Quseir.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/myot-hill"><span class="enc-name">Myot Hill</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Myot Hill is a small hill near Denny, south of Stirling in central Scotland, crowned by the remains of a hillfort.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/myra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/myra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Myra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Myra was a city of ancient Lycia, near modern Demre in southern Turkey, and one of the six largest members of the Lycian League.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/myrcinus"><span class="enc-name">Myrcinus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Myrcinus was an ancient town of the Edonians in Thrace, near the lower Strymon river and the later site of Amphipolis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/myrrh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/myrrh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Myrrh</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Myrrh is an aromatic resin obtained from trees native to north-east Africa and southern Arabia, prized in antiquity for incense, perfume and embalming.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/myrsilus"><span class="enc-name">Myrsilus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Myrsilus was a tyrant of Mytilene on Lesbos around 600 BCE, during the city's struggles between aristocratic factions.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/myscellus-of-rhypes"><span class="enc-name">Myscellus of Rhypes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Myscellus of Rhypes was the legendary founder (oikist) of Croton in southern Italy, around 710 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mysia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mysia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mysia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mysia is a historical region of north-western Anatolia, lying south of the Sea of Marmara and bordering the Troad, Lydia and Bithynia.</span></a></li></ul>
